        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning from Mistakes</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn from This Experience?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize audio and video content for study notes. Communication: Clarify misunderstood social cues in peer interactions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize the main points from an educational video on learning from mistakes by drawing symbols (e.g., drawing a red "X" for a mistake and a checkmark for learning).
Can clarify misunderstood social cues during discussions on personal growth by using facial expressions (e.g., showing "happy" for success and "sad" for a mistake).
Can summarize stories about overcoming challenges, using visuals and gestures (e.g., thumbs up for improvement and drawing a ladder for progress).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by displaying three large emoji flashcards: one with a sad face (for "mistake"), one with a thinking face (for "learning"), and one with a happy face (for "success"). Students are asked to briefly recall a time they felt like one of the emojis related to a challenge or a school task. The teacher prompts with, "Think about a time you made a mistake. What did you learn?" (2-3 minutes).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models summarizing a short personal anecdote about a mistake and the lesson learned, using gestures and facial expressions. For example, "Last week, I forgot my lesson plan at home (sad face, 'mistake'). I felt stressed, but then I quickly remembered the main points and taught the lesson (thinking face, 'learn'). So, I learned to always double-check my bag (happy face, 'success')." The teacher emphasizes using past simple ("forgot," "learned") and future tense ideas ("Next time, I will check carefully.") and clear pronunciation of -ed verbs (e.g., 'learned' /d/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the concept of "learning from mistakes" and the target vocabulary (e.g., 'mistake,' 'learn,' 'improve,' 'success').</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I make a mistake, does it mean I'm a bad student?" (No, it means you can learn and improve.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we use a red 'X' symbol for a mistake and a green checkmark for learning?" (Yes, we can use symbols to summarize ideas quickly.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A 2-3 minute educational video clip about "Learning from Your Mistakes: Tips for Growth" (e.g., from a YouTube channel aimed at teens). The video should present clear, actionable advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand the main points of an educational video and practice summarizing them using symbols and short notes. They will also recognize basic advice given in the video.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Before playing, ask: "What do you think this video will teach us about mistakes?" After the first viewing: "What is the main idea of the video?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Symbol Summary:</b> Students watch the video a second time. Their task is to take 'study notes' by drawing symbols to represent key advice or points mentioned in the video. For example, a lightbulb for "new ideas," a book for "study harder," a ladder for "progress." They also listen for words like <i>mistakes, improve, learn, habits, better, success</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 statements based on the video's content (e.g., "The video says we should ignore our mistakes." "Learning from mistakes makes us better students."). Students mark them True or False and correct the false ones by rephrasing them.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice summarizing information and giving advice using past simple, future tense, and "should." They will use vocabulary related to learning and improvement (e.g., <i>mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, feedback, progress, improvement, success, better, challenging</i>). Pronunciation of past tense verbs ending /d/, /ɪd/, /t/ will be reinforced, along with phonemic awareness of minimal pairs like /ð/ vs /d/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Summary Cloze:</b> Students receive a short paragraph summarizing the main points of the video watched in Stage 2, but with key vocabulary (e.g., <i>mistakes, improve, habits, better</i>) or verb tenses (past simple, future tense) missing. Students fill in the blanks. Teacher models pronunciation of -ed endings, e.g., "I *improved* /d/ my grades," "We *practiced* /t/ carefully," "They *wanted* /ɪd/ feedback." (5 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Advice Rephrasing:</b> In pairs, students receive cards with common school challenges (e.g., "I failed a test," "I didn't finish my homework"). For each card, Partner A states the problem using past simple ("I failed my English test last week"). Partner B must summarize the problem using different words and then give advice using "should" and future tense ("So, you didn't do well on your test. You should study more for future tests. Next time, you will manage your study time better."). The teacher encourages the use of facial expressions to clarify understanding or empathy (e.g., a concerned look when summarizing a challenge). (7 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Minimal Pair Sound Check:</b> The teacher quickly pronounces pairs of words and asks students to identify if they hear a difference (e.g., "They" /ðeɪ/ vs. "Day" /deɪ/, "Think" /θɪŋk/ vs. "Sink" /sɪŋk/, "Van" /væn/ vs. "Ban" /bæn/). Students raise their hands if they hear a difference. This helps to improve auditory comprehension critical for accurate mediation. (3 minutes)</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Future Self Video Project Explanation (Mini-Presentation):</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student takes a turn explaining the concept and content of their "Future Self Video" project to their group members. They do not show the actual video yet, but rather mediate its content verbally. They must:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describe two to three past <i>mistakes</i> they "made" (using past simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explain what they "learned" from these experiences (using past simple, e.g., "I learned to study harder." "I learned to ask for <i>feedback</i>.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Share what their 'future self' "will do" differently to show <i>improvement</i> and <i>progress</i> (using future tense, e.g., "Next time, I will *practice* /t/ more carefully." "I will set better <i>habits</i>.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use at least two visual aids or gestures as they explain, similar to the Learning Outcomes (e.g., drawing a quick "X" on a mini-whiteboard for a mistake, a thumbs-up for improvement, a ladder for progress). They should also use facial expressions to clarify understanding or convey emotion (e.g., a "sad" face for a past mistake, a "happy" face for future success).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group members listen and can ask clarifying questions, practicing the sociolinguistic competence of providing constructive feedback ("You should try to explain what happened more clearly.").</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging the use of target language and mediation strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Formative assessment strategy.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Peer Feedback and Summary:</b> After each student explains their Future Self Video project, their group members use a simple "Feedback Card" to provide constructive feedback. On the card, they must:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize in one sentence the main lesson learned by their peer (e.g., " [Name] learned to study more carefully.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Suggest one point for <i>improvement</i> regarding their peer's explanation or video idea (e.g., "You should add more details about how you will *improve* your *grades*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw one symbol or emoji that represents the core message of their peer's future self video (e.g., a brain for 'learning', a medal for 'success'). This directly assesses their ability to summarize using visuals.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of past tense verbs and target vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Integration of nouns (<i>mistakes, grades, progress</i>), verbs (<i>learn, improve</i>), adjectives (<i>better, challenging</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Clear explanation of past mistake, lesson learned, and future plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation Strategies:</b> Effective use of gestures, facial expressions, or symbols to convey meaning and clarify misunderstood cues.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Ability to give and receive constructive feedback appropriately.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How clearly did I explain my project using different tenses (past and future)?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use gestures or symbols to make my message easier to understand? Why is this important?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of summarizing your ideas or giving advice today, and what helped you to overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will using symbols and gestures help you remember what you *learned* /d/ about learning from <i>mistakes</i>?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which mediation activities worked best to help students summarize and clarify? Were the symbols and gestures effective for conveying meaning?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students effectively use past simple, future tense, and 'should' for reflection and advice? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, especially regarding clarifying social cues?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is Lesson 5, the culmination of the "What Can I Learn from This Experience?" theme and the 21st Century Skills Project. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, applying the skills of reflecting on experiences and setting goals for future improvement in a new context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>